    <h1>Cell Concentration</h1>


    <!-- THEORETICAL DESCRIPTION -->

    <div class="description">

        <p>
            A Bürker chamber is a counting chamber with a precisely defined
            grid and chamber depth. It allows the concentration of cells,
            spores, or other microscopic objects in a suspension to be
            estimated by counting the objects present in a known volume.
        </p>

        <p>
            The chamber has a defined depth of 0.1 mm. Because the dimensions
            of the counting areas are known, each area corresponds to a
            precisely defined volume of sample. Counting objects in several
            areas of the same size therefore allows their concentration to be
            calculated.
        </p>


        <h2>How does the calculation work?</h2>

        <p>
            First, the average number of objects per counted area is
            calculated:
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong>
                Average = total number of counted objects ÷ number of counted areas
            </strong>
        </p>

        <p>
            The average is then converted according to the size of the area
            counted. In this calculator, the final concentration is expressed
            as <strong>× 10⁶ objects/mL</strong>.
        </p>


        <h2>Bürker chamber geometry</h2>

        <p>
            In the Bürker grid used for this calculation, the relevant
            counting areas have the following dimensions:
        </p>

        <table>
            <tr>
                <th>Area</th>
                <th>Dimensions</th>
                <th>Relative conversion</th>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>Big square</td>
                <td>1/5 × 1/5 mm</td>
                <td>Average ÷ 4</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>Rectangle</td>
                <td>1/5 × 1/20 mm</td>
                <td>Average × 1</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
                <td>Small square</td>
                <td>1/20 × 1/20 mm</td>
                <td>Average × 4</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            These conversion factors arise from the different volumes
            represented by the three areas while the chamber depth remains
            constant. The big square contains four times the volume of the
            rectangle, while the small square contains one quarter of the
            volume of the rectangle. Therefore, the same number of objects
            corresponds to a four-times lower or four-times higher
            concentration, respectively.
        </p>


        <h2>What can be counted?</h2>

        <p>
            The same principle can be used for different types of samples,
            including:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>cells</li>
            <li>yeast or fungal spores</li>
            <li>other microscopic particles or objects</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            When several areas of the same size are counted, their total
            number is divided by the number of areas to obtain the average
            count per area.
        </p>


        <h2>Dilution factor</h2>

        <p>
            If the sample was diluted before counting, the concentration
            calculated from the Bürker chamber represents the diluted sample.
            To obtain the concentration in the original sample, the result
            is multiplied by the dilution factor.
        </p>

        <p>
            For example, if a sample was diluted 1:10 before counting, the
            dilution factor is <strong>10</strong>. If no dilution was
            performed, the dilution factor is <strong>1</strong>.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- CALCULATOR -->

    <div class="calculator">

        <h2>Calculate concentration</h2>


        <label for="total-count">
            Total number of counted objects
        </label>

        <input
            type="number"
            id="total-count"
            min="0"
            step="any"
            placeholder="e.g. 245"
        >


        <label for="shape">
            Which area did you count?
        </label>

        <select id="shape">

            <option value="b">
                Big square
            </option>

            <option value="r">
                Rectangle
            </option>

            <option value="s">
                Small square
            </option>

        </select>


        <label for="shapes-count">
            How many areas of this size did you count?
        </label>

        <input
            type="number"
            id="shapes-count"
            min="0"
            step="any"
            placeholder="e.g. 5"
        >


        <label for="dilution-factor">
            Dilution factor
        </label>

        <input
            type="number"
            id="dilution-factor"
            min="0"
            step="any"
            value="1"
            placeholder="e.g. 10"
        >

        <p style="font-size: 14px; color: #666; margin-top: 8px;">
            Enter 1 if the sample was not diluted.
            For a 1:10 dilution, enter 10.
        </p>


        <button onclick="calculateConcentration()">
            Calculate concentration
        </button>


        <div id="result"></div>

    </div>


    <!-- FORMULA -->

    <div class="formula">

        <h2>Calculation</h2>

        <p>
            The calculator performs the following steps:
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong>1.</strong>
            The total number of objects is divided by the number of counted
            areas to obtain the average.
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong>2.</strong>
            The average is converted according to the selected area.
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong>3.</strong>
            The dilution factor is applied.
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong>4.</strong>
            The final concentration is reported as:
        </p>

        <p style="text-align: center; font-size: 19px;">
            <strong>× 10⁶ objects/mL</strong>
        </p>

    </div>


